{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789515045,"nonce":"eqvR66SoBt0/+YpX8yoGSC3kzow=","title":"Python: Base.method(obj, ...) was a dynamic attribute read on None","message":"`Task.__init__(self, i, p, w, s, r)` in a subclass's constructor, and any `Class.method(obj, args)`, read the attribute off the class name as a value, which lowered as None: AttributeError 'NoneType' object has no attribute '__init__'. Now a call through a bare class name is the class's own method with the first argument as the receiver, typed like any other call, and its arguments are recorded for the method's parameters. A constructor never gets a dispatcher, and an override taking a different number of arguments raises TypeError in the dispatcher's arm instead of being called with the wrong count.","files":null}]}